# What the source distribution ships.
#
# For a security library the sdist is not a convenience copy of the wheel: it is the
# artifact a distribution packager (Debian, Fedora, Nix, Conda, an internal mirror)
# fetches in order to *re-verify from source* the claims this package makes. That means
# it has to carry a test suite that runs.
#
# 2.0.0's sdist half-shipped one. `tests/*.py` were present; `tests/conftest.py`,
# `tests/vectors/slcs1.json`, `docs/`, `examples/`, `CHANGELOG.md` and `SECURITY.md`
# were not. Anyone who unpacked it and ran pytest got a collection error followed by
# ~194 fixture errors - a suite that looks broken rather than one that is absent, which
# is the worst of the three options (all, none, some).
#
# The rule here is therefore: ship everything needed to reproduce the verification, and
# nothing that is a build or scratch artifact. tests/test_packaging.py enforces both
# halves - TestSdistContents checks the manifest, TestSdistIsSelfVerifying unpacks the
# built sdist and runs the suite out of it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Documents. SECURITY.md and CHANGELOG.md are referenced from the package metadata and
# from the threat-model tests; docs/ARCHITECTURE.md is the normative contract every
# module is written against, and tests/test_docs_execute.py executes its code blocks.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
include LICENSE
include README.md
include CHANGELOG.md
include SECURITY.md
include MANIFEST.in
graft docs

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The test suite, whole. conftest.py holds every backend, ledger and signing-key
# fixture in the project, and tests/vectors/slcs1.json is the frozen SLCS/1 byte
# format - the canonical serialization cannot be re-verified without it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
graft tests

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The runnable examples. tests/test_examples.py executes these, so they are part of
# the suite whether or not they read like documentation.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
graft examples

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The typing marker, which is package data and also belongs in the sdist.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
include signledger/py.typed

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `graft tests` sweeps up whatever a local run left behind, and __pycache__ is always
# there. This is deliberately the ONLY exclusion. distutils prints a warning for every
# pattern that matches nothing, and a release build that prints a screenful of warnings
# is a release build nobody reads - which is the same failure mode that let a broken
# manifest ship in the first place. The guard against everything else (a stray
# ledger.db, a .DS_Store, a coverage file) is
# tests/test_packaging.py::TestSdistContents::test_the_sdist_ships_no_build_or_scratch_artefact,
# which fails loudly on the real artifact instead of hiding the mistake behind a
# speculative pattern.
#
# (`python -m build` still prints this one warning once, during its "build wheel from
# sdist" pass: the unpacked sdist has no __pycache__ left to exclude, because this line
# worked. That is the exclusion succeeding, not failing.)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
global-exclude *.py[cod]
